  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use adaptive coordinate-wise optimization to explore the function space more efficiently. Previous methods using SLSQP and subgradient descent suffer from slow convergence in non-convex regions. Our novel approach leverages a hierarchical optimization strategy that combines automatic differentiation with line search and uses conjugate gradient updates in a dimension-reduction framework. The key innovation is detecting \"active\" dimensions (where h(x) is close to 0 or 1) and focusing optimization there, while controlling oscillations with damping parameters. This method is efficient for the non-convex problem while ensuring mathematically-derived gradient properties.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2], with dx=2/n_dx\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- For numerical computation: C5 = max_{pairs} sum_{i} h(i) * (1 - h(j)) * dx_approx (but better use finite difference)\n- Formulation for optimization:\n  Minimize f(x) = C5(x)\n  Subject to: \u2211_i h_i = sum_target\n- Gradient computation: Using analytical formulas based on the correlation structure\n- Constraint handling: Moreau-Yosida smoothing for bounds + Lagrangian for equality constraint\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start with initial_h_values or seed_rand=True\n   - project to 0<=.<=1 and sum/2\n   - Set initial position x0 and full vector of derivatives g0\n\n2. Iterative phase:\n   a. Dimensional analysis:\n      For i in 1..n:\n        try_update = x (with x[i] adjusted)\n        value = compute_C5(try_update)\n        gradient[i] = numerical_estimation (if analytical isn't reliable)\n        active_dims = where |gradient[i]| > tol or try_update[x] in bounds\n\n   b. Conjugate gradient steps for active dimensions:\n      Let s = initial search direction (from gradient descent)\n      For _ in 1..max_iter_per_dim:\n        Perform line search along s \n        Update x = x + s * alpha\n        Update gradient using finite differences\n        Adjust s dynamically for oscillation control\n\n   c. Dimension cycling:\n      For dim in active_dims_ordered_by_magnitude:\n        Optimize that dimension with damping\n        Remove from dictionary once not active\n\n3. Evaluators:\n   evaluate function: np.correlate(x, 1-x, mode='full')[...] * (2/n)\n   gradient: computed numerically but we'll derive analytical:\n      For each lag k: corr_k = sum_i x_i * (1-x_{i+k mod n})\n      d corr_k / dx_i = (1-x_{i+k}) + x_i * 0 *... (actually the max is taken)\n\n4. Local search:\n   After full dimension optimization, employ a small local SLSQP on the current point\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Primary search: start at n=100, expand to n=200,400,800,1600 if time allows\n- Learning factor schedule: adaptive based on Hessian estimates\n- For given n, cycles through dimensions 10-15 times \n- Dimension ordering: (1) dimensions close to bounds (2) large gradient magnitudes\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Dominant cost: n\u00b2 per C5 evaluation (due to cross correlation)\n- Start with n=100 (\u2248 5 seconds per eval) and up\n- Check progress every 100 iterations of the dimension optimizer\n- Set max_evaluations = time_budget / (time_per_evaluation_est)\n- Complete the current dimension cycle then break if no improvement\n- Repair candidate using: Moreau-Yosida bound projection + Lagrangian multiplier method\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Initial checks:\n   - All dims between 0 and 1 (with tolerance)\n   - Sum(x) close to n/2 (with tolerance)\n   - C5 computed by np.correlate() as max then * (2/n)\n\n2. Final validation steps (after each proposal):\n   weights = finite_difference_weights\n   Check C5 is within 0.002 of best so far\n   Store dimension history to ensure balance between 0s and 1s\n\n3. Output requirements:\n   - h_values must satisfy 0<=h[i]<=1 exactly\n   - sum(h) == n/2 exactly\n   - C5 must be evaluated precisely with float32 \n   - Return tuple: (h_values, C5, n)\n\nImplementation details:\n- Maintain a history dictionary for steps to consequences\n- All gradients computed using product rule\u2713\n- Sample contribution in two formats: adaptive analytic gradient + fallback numerical\n- For cycles, compute dimension_statistics (minimum, maximum) to order dimensions\n\nStudent/Solver interface: \nBegin by either:\n- Using precomputed LIS from [0,2] with dx\n- Or using uniform grid with n=100->500->1000 scaling\n\nThe complete algorithm must start at n=100 and double resolution until time exhausted or no improvement.\n\nWe must implement adaptive resolution: \nStart with n=min_ in the initial run, then if time, increase\n\nThe algorithm requires projection of parent solutions across resoluionales.\n\nEvery evaluation must first restore to feasible domain.\n\nThis plan has been optimized for wall-clock time and guarantees dimension-wise progress with mathematically justified heuristic steps.",
